  "reasoning": "The patient presents with two key symptom clusters: dermatological (skin peeling and rashes on arms, legs, and back) and musculoskeletal (joint pain). This specific combination strongly points to psoriasis, particularly psoriatic arthritis, which characteristically involves inflammatory skin lesions (rashes, peeling/scaly patches) alongside joint inflammation. Reference examples [1] and [7] explicitly link rashes/peeling skin on the limbs and trunk with joint pain to a diagnosis of psoriasis. Other considered candidates like arthritis lack the prominent dermatological features, allergy presents with respiratory/ocular symptoms, and drug reaction typically involves different systemic signs (like twitching) without the chronic peeling rash pattern described. Thus, psoriasis is the single unit established by the facts.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]psoriasis[/DIAGNOSIS]"
